      "width": 2032,
      "height": 2506,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "blmcalifornia",
      "description": "Three hundred years ago, California’s Central Valley was vast grassland where antelope and elk grazed and wildflowers swept the spring landscape. Today, amid urban and agriculture development, a remnant remains in the Carrizo Plain National Monument.\nCarrizo Plain National Monument is one of the best kept secrets in California. Only a few hours from Los Angeles, the Carrizo Plain offers visitors a rare chance to be alone with nature. Some visitors say you can \"hear the silence.\" The plain is home to diverse communities of wildlife and plant species including several listed as threatened or endangered and is an area culturally important to Native Americans. \n\nPhoto by Jesse Pluim, BLM",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 28 June 2021, 07:44:14",
